[1:46:42 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  Analyzing commit: fix(ci): pin @semantic-release/github to 11.x to unblock releases

Backports the fix already on main (b4da84a5dd), preventatively — this branch has
not released since the problem appeared, but it is in the same state stable/9 was
when its v9.1.3 run crashed uploading the GitHub release asset:

  InvalidArgumentError: invalid content-length header (UND_ERR_INVALID_ARG)
  POST https://uploads.github.com/.../releases/<id>/assets

@semantic-release/github 12.x depends on undici ^7, which rejects the numeric
content-length header octokit sets when uploading a release asset. 11.0.6 is the
last release before that dependency and uses undici 6.x. This branch had no
override and resolved to 12.0.2.

Deliberately overriding rather than downgrading semantic-release itself: PR #377
previously downgraded 25 -> 24.2.9 for this, which dragged @semantic-release/npm
from 13.x to 12.x and broke npm OIDC trusted publishing (EINVALIDNPMTOKEN). The
override keeps semantic-release 25.0.8 and @semantic-release/npm 13.1.3.

Verified after install: @semantic-release/github resolves to 11.0.6, no 12.x
remains anywhere in the tree, and no undici v7 is present.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
[1:46:42 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  The release type for the commit is patch
[1:46:42 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  Analyzing commit: test(security): align the static slot guard with the script's whitespace matching

Mirrors the fix applied to the stable/9 PR after a Copilot review note. The
static regression check matched only an exact `slot: {}`, while
scripts/postprocess-harden-client-params.ts was updated to match `\{\s*\}`. A
slot emitted as `{ }` is just as vulnerable, and the guard let that shape
through.

Confirmed against a `{ }` initialiser: the static check passed while only the
two behavioural tests failed. The suite still went red overall, so the gap was
not a false green, but the static guard is the precise signal and should not
have a blind spot the script does not. It now fails too — 3 of 3 rather than 2
of 3 — and all three still pass once hardened.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
[1:46:42 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  The commit should not trigger a release
[1:46:42 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  Analyzing commit: fix(security): scope the hardening check to the located initialiser

Addresses Copilot review on #387. Two defects in the patch script:

1. The `alreadyHardened` guard scanned the whole file. Any unrelated
   `Object.create(null)` elsewhere in the template would satisfy it, so the
   script would exit 0 reporting "already hardened" while leaving the real
   initialiser vulnerable — exactly the silent-no-op failure this script exists
   to prevent. Reproduced against the previous version: with a decoy
   `const unrelatedDefaults = { body: Object.create(null), ... }` above
   buildClientParams and a vulnerable `body: {}` initialiser, it printed
   "nothing to patch" and left the sink in place.

   The initialiser is now located first and every check runs against that block
   alone.

2. The rewrite matched only an exact `{}`, so a formatting-only change such as
   `{ }` would have tripped the completeness check and failed the build. Now
   matches `\{\s*\}`.

Per-slot handling also replaces the old aggregate count, so a partially
hardened initialiser is patched correctly rather than rejected, while any slot
that is neither empty nor already hardened still fails loudly and names itself.

Verified across five cases: already hardened (no-op), vulnerable (patches 4),
whitespace `{ }` variants (patches 4), decoy elsewhere plus vulnerable
initialiser (patches 4 — previously skipped), and an unrecognised slot shape
(exits 1, naming the slot).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
[1:46:42 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  The release type for the commit is patch
[1:46:42 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  Analyzing commit: test(security): make the allowExtra prototype-pollution case actually assert

Mirrors the fix applied to the stable/9 PR after Copilot review. The allowExtra
regression test passed against the vulnerable implementation, so it asserted
nothing. Two separate defects:

1. It only checked Object.prototype. The write is
   `params.query['__proto__'] = value`, which substitutes the prototype of the
   slot object, not the global one — so the assertion held either way. Now
   asserts the slot itself keeps a null prototype and does not inherit the
   payload.

2. The payload was an object literal, `{ __proto__: { ... } }`. That sets the
   literal's own prototype and creates no own key, so Object.entries yielded []
   and the allowExtra branch never executed at all. Now built with JSON.parse,
   which produces a genuine own property named `__proto__` — and is how this
   actually arrives in practice, from a parsed request body.

Verified against the vulnerable implementation: previously 2 of 3 tests failed,
now all 3 do. All 3 pass once the hardening is applied.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
[1:46:42 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  The commit should not trigger a release
[1:46:42 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  Analyzing commit: fix(security): harden generated buildClientParams against prototype pollution

Fixes CVE-2026-48819 / GHSA-hhx9-57xq-r5rw on stable/8 without changing the
published API surface.

src/gen/core/params.gen.ts is a runtime template copied verbatim out of
@hey-api/openapi-ts into the generated SDK, so it ships to consumers. It
initialises each request slot with a plain object literal and then writes
caller-controlled keys into those slots — via the `$<slot>_` extra-prefix branch
and via `allowExtra`. A key of `$query___proto__` therefore reaches
`params.query['__proto__'] = value`, invoking the `__proto__` setter and
substituting the object's prototype with caller-supplied data. Applications that
forward user-supplied request parameters into a generated client method — proxies,
BFF layers, API gateways — are reachable. The exploit was confirmed against this
branch before the fix.

Upstream fixed this in 0.97.3 by giving each slot a null prototype. Rather than
bump the generator, this applies exactly that upstream change via a new
postprocess step, scripts/postprocess-harden-client-params.ts, wired into
_pipeline:generate directly after generate:sdk:

    body/headers/path/query: {}  ->  Object.create(null)

Bumping @hey-api/openapi-ts is deliberately avoided. This branch is pinned to
0.82.5; reaching a patched generator means crossing many minors, and the same
exercise measured on stable/9 (0.86.12 -> 0.97.3) left the exported symbol set
identical but changed 34 type declarations re-exported from
@hey-api/client-fetch — several breaking, including BodySerializer switching from
`any` to `unknown`, ErrInterceptor gaining `| undefined` on its response and
request parameters, `request`/`response` becoming optional, and a changed SseFn
signature. That is unacceptable on a stable branch, so the fix is applied to the
generated output instead.

The script is idempotent, refuses to emit partially hardened output, and fails
loudly if the generator's template shape changes rather than silently no-opping.
Once this branch moves to a generator >= 0.97.3 it becomes a no-op.

Adds tests/client-params-prototype-pollution.test.ts as a class-of-defect guard:
a static check that no slot regresses to a plain object literal, plus two
behavioural checks that neither the `$<slot>___proto__` nor the bare `__proto__`
(allowExtra) path pollutes Object.prototype. Verified failing before the fix and
passing after.

Verified: type surface byte-identical — the built d.ts chunk keeps the same
content hash (index-BvRQQCn2.d.ts, 598.9K) before and after. Test suite matches
this branch's baseline exactly; the 3 pre-existing Windows-specific failures in
tests/mtls.test.ts fail identically on unmodified stable/8.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
